data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In .NET 10, the Cosmos DB provider allowed synchronous LINQ operations via an explicit **sync-over-async opt-in** (e.g., `CosmosOptionsExtension` or setting `EnableSyncExecution`). In .NET 11, **that opt-in is removed**. All synchronous query/save operations on the Cosmos provider now throw `InvalidOperationException` unconditionally.

Every call in your repository is affected:

| Sync (breaks) | Async replacement |
|---|---|
| `.ToList()` | `.ToListAsync()` |
| `.First(...)` | `.FirstAsync(...)` |
| `.Count()` | `.CountAsync()` |
| `.SaveChanges()` | `.SaveChangesAsync()` |

## Fix

Convert to async throughout:

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- `Add()` stays synchronous — it only tracks the entity in memory, no I/O.
- All callers up the chain must also become `async`/`await` (controllers, services, etc.).
- Remove any `EnableSyncExecution` / sync-over-async configuration — it's no longer recognized and may throw on startup.
